From: freeman@netcom.com (Jay Reynolds Freeman)
Date: Mon, 10 Oct 1994 18:02:40 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-175] Animal intelligence

    There was a thread here on animal intelligence a year or so ago; I
have just run across an interesting new data point:

I cite the following reference:

    Baptista, Luis F., 1994.  "Instinct and Imitation:  Cultural Traditions
      in Birds", in _Pacific_Discovery_ _47_, no. 4, (Fall, 1994), 8-15.


            Green-backed herons ... often exhibit the remarkable
        behavior of fishing with bait or a "lure." ...

            Although green-backed herons are widespread, this
        behavior has been observed only in the southern United
        States, western Africa, and Japan. ...

            Juveniles were less successful at fishing than adults.
        This was partly because, unlike adults, young herons did
        not crouch after throwing the bait and must have been
        more conspicuous.  Juveniles also tended to use lures --
        sticks and feathers -- that were too long, while an
        adult was seen to hold six-to-seven-centimeter twigs
        under its foot, then break off small pieces with its
        bill before using them as tools.  Truly a case of tool
        using!


    To which I add, that this is not only a case of tool using, but --
in the last instance described -- a case of tool-*making*, as previously
reported, I believe, only in apes and humans.  And it seems to be
learned, not instinctive.  And a green heron has a brain perhaps the
size of a walnut, or maybe not so large.

    I have said before, we live on a wonderful planet, and most people
don't even know it exists.

                                                --  Jay Freeman


PS:  _Pacific_Discovery_ is the "slick", _Scientific_American_-level
     publication of the California Academy of Sciences.

