From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 10 Oct 94 19:19:26 EDT
Subject: [#94-10-176] Charlie and Rheingold (revisited)

Craig Presson wrote 10/10/94: <Now, here's a serious question about
Rheingold-style rhetorical questions ("Should we do [whatever] just because it's
technically feasible?"). How do we counter this type of thinking? After all,
there is a threat in there, especially coming from a Liberal: "Should *we* do
something?" implies that *we* can decide not to, i.e., that the Vox Populi can
enforce limits on our lives, on what risks we can take. OTOH, we know that there
are risks no rational person would take ("Oh, what the h*ll, I'm pretty sure
this formula will yield grey goo, but I'll make it anyway"), and we don't want
to be seen as callously dismissing real dangers where they do arise.>

"Should" questions: (1) it's theoretically possible, should we develop it? or
(2) it's been developed, should we use it?  It sounds like the domain of moral
philosophy to me.  The questions can be answered case-by-case, tailored to
either antagonize, placate, fool or engage the questioner depending on which
direction the answerer would like to see the discussion proceed to.  I don't
think there's any rule of thumb involved here, it's not the "type of thinking"
that's a problem; it's a question of what actions will people take who don't
like what they see going on.

The Vox Populi is a scary scenario, do-gooders frequently get out of hand. The
"risks no rational person would take" is also a scary scenario because
rationality is a sliding scale of thought.  What's irrational to you and I may
be rational to someone else further down the scale.  We have to deal with people
on a continuous scale of consciousness and an unlimited diversity of individual
purposes.  Technology, once developed, tends not to go away, so in some sense,
it may be more practical to ask developmental questions before development
rather than usage questions after the fact.  These questions always get back to
specific discussions of the pros and cons of purposes lying behind the actions
required to develop technology (the pros and cons of the means is fair game,
also).

A useful principle to keep in mind in these discussions of specifics is: It is
easier to pick a direction in life by knowing what to run from, than it is to
pick a direction to run to without knowing where you'll end up.  There are many
extropic paths available that all generally follow the Principles v2.5, it is
difficult to see which path might be best.  It is far easier to see which paths
to avoid by observing the negation of the Principles v2.5.  Just avoid any
actions that would lead to the following outcomes:

---------
Bounded Expansion        Boundless Contraction     Bounded Contraction
Non-Self-Transformation  Self-Stagnation           Non-Self-Stagnation
Static Optimism          Dynamic Pessimism         Static Pessimism
Dumb Technology          Intelligent Uselessness   Dumb Uselessness
Predetermined Order      Spontaneous Disorder      Predetermined Disorder

