From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Date: Tue, 11 Oct 1994 13:09:35 -0500 (CDT)
Subject: [#94-10-180] Challenge to uploaders

Ed Regis writes:
 
> One thing has always bothered me about the uploading idea.  From what I
> understand, the idea is that some fab computer of the future will run a
> simulation of your total brain/mental structure, thereby bringing you to
> consciousness in and among the computer chips (or their successors).  My
> problem is that simulations normally don't produce the physical attributes of
> the things simulated: a simulation of a hurricane, for example, isn't wet
> inside the computer; there's no rain and/or wind in there.  So how do you
> know that your simulated mental structures will give rise to consciousness,
> or anything like it?  

Wetness, rain, and wind are *defined* to be interactions of physical
objects -- physical water molecules, physical gas molecules, etc,
undergoing certain physical actions.  The very definitions of the
terms make them inaccessable to a "simulation", since it has no
physical water molecules to move around.

For this to be bothersome for consciousness of an upload, it has
to be the case that consciousness is *defined* similarly as being
particular physical interactions of particular physical objects
that are inaccessable to the "simulation".  I cannot see a
justification for such a definition of consciousness.  For example,
the following reasoning:

  Our brains are conscious
  Our brains have particular physical properties
  -----------------
  Consciousness requires particular physical properties

is fallacious.  I see no reason to suppose that consciousness
requires certain specific physical interactions; when I attempt
(clumsily, no doubt) to define consciousness, I do not find myself
forced or even tempted to use the language of molecular
biology.

derek


