From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Date:     Wed, 12 Oct 94 0:14:40 GMT
Subject: [#94-10-188] Challenge to uploaders

 Derek Zahn <derek@cs.wisc.edu> wrote:
>
>For this to be bothersome for consciousness of an upload, it has
>to be the case that consciousness is *defined* similarly as being
>particular physical interactions of particular physical objects
>that are inaccessable to the "simulation".  I cannot see a
>justification for such a definition of consciousness.  For example,
>the following reasoning:
>
>  Our brains are conscious
>  Our brains have particular physical properties
>  -----------------
>  Consciousness requires particular physical properties
>
>is fallacious.  I see no reason to suppose that consciousness
>requires certain specific physical interactions; when I attempt
>(clumsily, no doubt) to define consciousness, I do not find myself
>forced or even tempted to use the language of molecular
>biology.
>
The syllogism is fallacious, but so is a syllogism which concludes
that consciousness does *not* require particular physical properties--
we just don't know yet whether consciousness is more like a knot
which is much the same no matter what kind of rope it's made of
or more like an economy which (so far as we know) can't be well
simulated by anything simpler than itself. (I realize that I'm
assuming that an upload will be based on a simplified version
of the brain, but the versions that I've seen of uploading do
assume that there's a lot of detail about brains that can be
dispensed with.)

For what it's worth, I gather that people have assumed that minds
are like the most complicated machines available--clocks, hydraulic
systems, switchboards....and now computers are the standard metaphor.
There's no reason to think that computers are actually a good
comparison, and some reason (computers and people have wildly
different strong and weak points) to think that people aren't
very much like computers.

I think it's worthwhile to try to simulate human abilities and
consciousness on computers--it's possible to learn a lot that
way--but I think it's at least as likely that we'll get something
new and (and quite possibly wonderful) as that we'll get anything
much like ourselves.

Nancy Lebovitz

